.br
.SS "Basic usage"
Before you can do anything else, you need to define a schema\&. For the purposes of this guide, we'll use a simple object schema\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import * as z from "zod/v4";

const Player = z\&.object({
  username: z\&.string(),
  xp: z\&.number(),
});
.fi
.PP
.SS "Parsing data"
Given any Zod schema, use \fR\&.parse\fP to validate an input\&. If it's valid, Zod returns a strongly-typed \fIdeep clone\fP of the input\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
Player\&.parse({ username: "billie", xp: 100 });
// => returns { username: "billie", xp: 100 }
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBNote\fP — If your schema uses certain asynchronous APIs like \fRasync\fP \fBrefinements\fP or \fBtransforms\fP, you'll need to use the \fR\&.parseAsync()\fP method instead\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = z\&.string()\&.refine(async (val) => val\&.length <= 8);

await schema\&.parseAsync("hello");
// => "hello"
.fi
.PP
.SS "Handling errors"
When validation fails, the \fR\&.parse()\fP method will throw a \fRZodError\fP instance with granular information about the validation issues\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
try {
  Player\&.parse({ username: 42, xp: "100" });
} catch (err) {
  if (err instanceof z\&.ZodError) {
    err\&.issues;
    /* [
      {
        expected: 'string',
        code: 'invalid_type',
        path: [ 'username' ],
        message: 'Invalid input: expected string'
      },
      {
        expected: 'number',
        code: 'invalid_type',
        path: [ 'xp' ],
        message: 'Invalid input: expected number'
      }
    ] */
  }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
To avoid a \fRtry/catch\fP block, you can use the \fR\&.safeParse()\fP method to get back a plain result object containing either the successfully parsed data or a \fRZodError\fP\&. The result type is a \fRdiscriminated union\fP, so you can handle both cases conveniently\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const result = Player\&.safeParse({ username: 42, xp: "100" });
if (!result\&.success) {
  result\&.error; // ZodError instance
} else {
  result\&.data; // { username: string; xp: number }
}
.fi
.PP

.PP
\fBNote\fP — If your schema uses certain asynchronous APIs like \fRasync\fP \fBrefinements\fP or \fBtransforms\fP, you'll need to use the \fR\&.safeParseAsync()\fP method instead\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const schema = z\&.string()\&.refine(async (val) => val\&.length <= 8);

await schema\&.safeParseAsync("hello");
// => { success: true; data: "hello" }
.fi
.PP
.SS "Inferring types"
Zod infers a static type from your schema definitions\&. You can extract this type with the \fRz\&.infer<>\fP utility and use it however you like\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
const Player = z\&.object({
  username: z\&.string(),
  xp: z\&.number(),
});

// extract the inferred type
type Player = z\&.infer<typeof Player>;

// use it in your code
const player: Player = { username: "billie", xp: 100 };
.fi
.PP

.PP
In some cases, the input & output types of a schema can diverge\&. For instance, the \fR\&.transform()\fP API can convert the input from one type to another\&. In these cases, you can extract the input and output types independently:

.PP
.PP
.nf
const mySchema = z\&.string()\&.transform((val) => val\&.length);

type MySchemaIn = z\&.input<typeof mySchema>;
// => string

type MySchemaOut = z\&.output<typeof mySchema>; // equivalent to z\&.infer<typeof mySchema>
// number
.fi
.PP
 
